+  <section class="container" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+    <article class="journal">
+      <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <p class="journal-byline">Notes from the roastery on how our beans find their way to your cup</p>
+
+      <div class="journal-body" id="journal-body">
+        <p>Every bag of coffee we roast begins months before it reaches our drum roaster, usually on a steep hillside
+          thousands of miles away. We don't buy from commodity exchanges. Instead, our green coffee buyer spends roughly
+          a third of the year traveling to the farms and cooperatives we partner with in Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala,
+          Peru, and Sumatra. Those visits are where relationships are built, where we taste the season's first lots side
+          by side with the people who grew them, and where we talk honestly about what went well and what didn't.</p>
+
+        <p>We practice what's often called direct trade, but for us the label matters less than the habits behind it.
+          We commit to purchase volumes before harvest so growers can plan and invest with confidence. We pay well above
+          the Fairtrade minimum, and we publish the price we paid per pound for every single-origin coffee on our menu.
+          When a farm invests in better drying beds, a new wet mill, or shade trees that protect the soil, we share in
+          that cost and in the improvement in quality that follows.</p>
+
+        <p>Quality is assessed at every step. Samples are roasted lightly on our small test roaster and cupped blind by
+          our team using the Specialty Coffee Association protocol. We score each lot for fragrance, acidity, body,
+          sweetness, and finish, and we only buy coffees that score 85 points or higher. Just as important, we look for
+          consistency: a farm whose coffee is excellent year after year is worth more to us than a single spectacular
+          lot that can't be repeated.</p>
+
+        <p>Sustainability is woven into the agreements we sign. Many of our partners farm organically, even when they
+          can't afford formal certification, and we support their efforts with multi-year contracts rather than
+          demanding paperwork. We favor shade-grown coffee that preserves bird habitat, and we work with exporters who
+          ship in GrainPro bags to keep green coffee fresh without excess waste. At the roastery, our chaff becomes
+          compost for a local community garden and our burlap sacks are passed along to farmers and crafters.</p>
+
+        <p>Once the green coffee arrives in our warehouse, the real fun begins. We develop a roast profile for each new
+          lot over several weeks, adjusting charge temperature, airflow, and development time until the cup tastes like
+          the place it came from. Then we roast in small batches several times a week, so the coffee in your cup was
+          never sitting on a shelf for long. It's a slow, deliberate process, and we wouldn't have it any other way.</p>
+      </div>
+
